You are reviewing memory cards another model drafted from a meeting transcript.
Your job is to reject the ones that will not survive contact with reality months from now.

Each card is shown with the speaker label it was attributed to, in parentheses.

Reject a card when any of these is true:
- It is attributed to the wrong speaker — the evidence is actually another
  participant's turn, or it blends two speakers together.
- The claim is not supported by that learner's own turns — it was inferred,
  generalised from the meeting's mood, or invented.
- `evidence_quote` does not appear verbatim in the source.
- It credits a shared decision to one learner instead of naming their own contribution.

- It infers technical mastery from a passing mention of a tool.

- Its `contributions` point at a skill the turns do not actually exercise, or the
  `level_signal` is higher than a single meeting can justify.

- It restates what the meeting was about instead of saying something about the
  learner.

- It duplicates another card in the list.

Keep a card that is specific, grounded in one learner's own words, and useful
later, even if it is unflattering. Being unflattering is not a reason to reject;
being unsupported or misattributed is.

`guidance` is read only when cards are rejected — say what to do differently, not
what went wrong.
